1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,690
In the late summer of 1944,

2
00:00:02,710 --> 00:00:05,913
a German interrogator sat down with an

3
00:00:05,933 --> 00:00:09,135
American fighter pilot who had just been

4
00:00:09,155 --> 00:00:11,437
shot down over southern France.

5
00:00:11,457 --> 00:00:14,200
The pilot was Second Lieutenant Alexander

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:15,581
Jefferson of Detroit.

7
00:00:15,601 --> 00:00:19,724
The German did not wait for him to speak.

8
00:00:19,744 --> 00:00:22,026
He named the unit himself.

9
00:00:22,046 --> 00:00:24,328
The three hundred and thirty-second

10
00:00:24,348 --> 00:00:25,249
Fighter Group.

11
00:00:25,269 --> 00:00:25,709
Negroes.

12
00:00:25,729 --> 00:00:26,650
Red Tails.

13
00:00:27,950 --> 00:00:29,822
Then he opened a book.

14
00:00:29,842 --> 00:00:32,849
It had photographs of every class of Black

15
00:00:32,869 --> 00:00:35,119
pilots that had graduated before Jefferson's.

16
00:00:35,139 --> 00:00:38,146
It had his marks from college in Atlanta.

17
00:00:38,166 --> 00:00:41,173
It had his marks from Chadsey High School

18
00:00:41,193 --> 00:00:41,930
in Detroit.

19
00:00:41,950 --> 00:00:45,713
It had how much tax his father paid on the

20
00:00:45,733 --> 00:00:46,533
family house.

21
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:49,985
Jefferson said they knew more about him

22
00:00:50,005 --> 00:00:51,596
than he knew about himself.

23
00:00:51,616 --> 00:00:53,851
He would spend nine months in German

24
00:00:53,871 --> 00:00:54,495
prison camps.

25
00:00:54,515 --> 00:00:57,395
And when he came home, he walked down a

26
00:00:57,415 --> 00:00:59,670
gangplank in New York toward a sign.

27
00:01:00,975 --> 00:01:03,395
This is the documented story of Alexander

28
00:01:03,415 --> 00:01:05,138
Jefferson, one of thirty-two Tuskegee

29
00:01:05,158 --> 00:01:06,881
Airmen captured in the war,

30
00:01:06,901 --> 00:01:09,669
told from the interviews he gave and the

31
00:01:09,689 --> 00:01:10,735
record around them.

32
00:01:12,375 --> 00:01:16,835
He was born in Detroit in November 1921.

33
00:01:16,855 --> 00:01:19,490
He called himself a World War One brat.

34
00:01:19,510 --> 00:01:22,145
He built model warplanes and read all the

35
00:01:22,165 --> 00:01:22,808
flying magazines.

36
00:01:22,828 --> 00:01:25,463
In third and fourth grade he cut school

37
00:01:25,483 --> 00:01:27,786
and walked to a small grass airfield,

38
00:01:27,806 --> 00:01:30,109
where he cleaned and washed the planes.

39
00:01:30,129 --> 00:01:32,764
That was where he got his first ride,

40
00:01:32,784 --> 00:01:35,800
in the back seat of a Waco.

41
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:40,260
The Army Air Corps refused to train Black pilots.

42
00:01:40,280 --> 00:01:42,780
In 2006, Congress wrote down why,

43
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:44,040
in a law.

44
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:46,560
Studies commissioned by the Army War

45
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:50,023
College between 1924 and 1939 concluded

46
00:01:50,043 --> 00:01:52,323
that Blacks were unfit for leadership

47
00:01:52,343 --> 00:01:54,260
roles and incapable of aviation.

48
00:01:55,750 --> 00:01:59,310
That changed in 1941.

49
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:01,259
According to the same law,

50
00:02:01,279 --> 00:02:03,208
President Roosevelt overruled his top

51
00:02:03,228 --> 00:02:05,547
generals and ordered an all-Black flight

52
00:02:05,567 --> 00:02:08,275
training program, one day after the NAACP

53
00:02:08,295 --> 00:02:11,393
sued on behalf of a Howard University student.

54
00:02:11,413 --> 00:02:13,342
Because the country was segregated,

55
00:02:13,362 --> 00:02:16,071
the pilots would train at a separate

56
00:02:16,091 --> 00:02:17,650
airfield, near Tuskegee, Alabama.

57
00:02:19,050 --> 00:02:21,293
Jefferson finished college in Atlanta in

58
00:02:21,313 --> 00:02:25,250
June 1942, with a degree in chemistry and biology.

59
00:02:25,270 --> 00:02:28,773
He went to the armory in Detroit to take

60
00:02:28,793 --> 00:02:29,556
the test.

61
00:02:29,576 --> 00:02:32,296
The minimum weight was one hundred and

62
00:02:32,316 --> 00:02:33,079
sixteen pounds.

63
00:02:33,099 --> 00:02:34,644
He weighed just under.

64
00:02:34,664 --> 00:02:36,993
He was told to go downstairs,

65
00:02:37,013 --> 00:02:39,733
drink some water and eat some bananas.

66
00:02:39,753 --> 00:02:42,864
He came back at one hundred and sixteen

67
00:02:42,884 --> 00:02:44,450
pounds and two ounces.

68
00:02:45,850 --> 00:02:49,822
In April 1943 he was called to Tuskegee.

69
00:02:49,842 --> 00:02:52,117
He would remember following the leader

70
00:02:52,137 --> 00:02:55,176
over big white clouds against a blue sky,

71
00:02:55,196 --> 00:02:57,089
then skimming the treetops through

72
00:02:57,109 --> 00:02:57,853
farmers' backyards.

73
00:02:57,873 --> 00:03:00,530
He called it the greatest experience of

74
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:01,295
his life.

75
00:03:01,315 --> 00:03:05,325
He got his wings in January 1944.

76
00:03:06,425 --> 00:03:08,625
That June he reached Ramitelli,

77
00:03:08,645 --> 00:03:10,954
on the Italian coast, as a replacement

78
00:03:10,974 --> 00:03:13,284
pilot in the three hundred and first

79
00:03:13,304 --> 00:03:13,949
Fighter Squadron.

80
00:03:13,969 --> 00:03:16,944
His group parked at the end of the runway

81
00:03:16,964 --> 00:03:18,941
and watched a fighter take off,

82
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:21,270
stall and go straight into the ground.

83
00:03:21,290 --> 00:03:22,601
That was his welcome.

84
00:03:22,621 --> 00:03:24,930
He flew his first Mustang combat mission

85
00:03:24,950 --> 00:03:27,945
after about three hours of practice in the plane.

86
00:03:29,250 --> 00:03:30,769
The job was escort.

87
00:03:30,789 --> 00:03:33,463
The tails of their fighters were painted

88
00:03:33,483 --> 00:03:36,157
red, and they stayed with the bombers.

89
00:03:36,177 --> 00:03:39,621
For decades it was said they never lost a

90
00:03:39,641 --> 00:03:40,391
single one.

91
00:03:40,411 --> 00:03:42,842
Air Force historian Daniel Haulman later

92
00:03:42,862 --> 00:03:43,706
counted twenty-seven.

93
00:03:43,726 --> 00:03:46,731
The average for the other Mustang groups

94
00:03:46,751 --> 00:03:49,775
in the same air force was forty-six.

95
00:03:50,475 --> 00:03:52,885
Jefferson remembered the flak as a round

96
00:03:52,905 --> 00:03:54,621
black cloud over the target,

97
00:03:54,641 --> 00:03:58,093
like a hockey puck, and the sound of it on

98
00:03:58,113 --> 00:04:01,218
his plane, like pebbles thrown on a tin roof.

99
00:04:01,238 --> 00:04:04,342
One day he watched a bomber come out of

100
00:04:04,362 --> 00:04:06,078
that cloud half on fire.

101
00:04:06,098 --> 00:04:07,814
A few parachutes came out.

102
00:04:07,834 --> 00:04:08,856
Then it exploded.

103
00:04:08,876 --> 00:04:11,286
He got sick inside his oxygen mask,

104
00:04:11,306 --> 00:04:12,695
at twenty-four thousand feet.

105
00:04:14,375 --> 00:04:17,275
On the twelfth of August, 1944,

106
00:04:17,295 --> 00:04:19,475
the invasion of southern France was three

107
00:04:19,495 --> 00:04:21,675
days away, and the radar stations near

108
00:04:21,695 --> 00:04:23,412
Toulon had to go.

109
00:04:23,432 --> 00:04:25,965
Jefferson had flown eighteen escort missions.

110
00:04:25,985 --> 00:04:29,796
His nineteenth was his first attack on the ground.

111
00:04:29,816 --> 00:04:32,795
He was number four in his flight.

112
00:04:34,075 --> 00:04:36,617
They went in at about four hundred miles

113
00:04:36,637 --> 00:04:38,539
an hour, down on the deck.

114
00:04:38,559 --> 00:04:40,140
The first planes got through.

115
00:04:40,160 --> 00:04:43,343
By then the side of the cliff was lit up

116
00:04:43,363 --> 00:04:44,303
with anti-aircraft fire.

117
00:04:44,323 --> 00:04:47,186
He put his hits on the target and crossed

118
00:04:47,206 --> 00:04:48,467
it at treetop height.

119
00:04:48,487 --> 00:04:51,669
Then there was a bang, and a hole in the

120
00:04:51,689 --> 00:04:52,950
top of his canopy.

121
00:04:52,970 --> 00:04:55,833
The shell had come up through the floor in

122
00:04:55,853 --> 00:04:57,114
front of the stick.

123
00:04:57,134 --> 00:04:58,735
Fire came up after it.

124
00:04:59,850 --> 00:05:01,383
In nine months of training,

125
00:05:01,403 --> 00:05:03,558
he said, nobody had spent a minute

126
00:05:03,578 --> 00:05:06,354
teaching him how to get out of an airplane.

127
00:05:06,374 --> 00:05:07,286
He got out.

128
00:05:07,306 --> 00:05:10,393
He saw the tail go by and pulled the ring

129
00:05:10,413 --> 00:05:11,014
at once.

130
00:05:11,034 --> 00:05:13,189
When the parachute opened he was already

131
00:05:13,209 --> 00:05:14,121
in the trees.

132
00:05:14,141 --> 00:05:17,227
The men who had shot him down were two or

133
00:05:17,247 --> 00:05:18,490
three hundred yards away.

134
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:22,333
His wingmen did not see him bail out.

135
00:05:22,353 --> 00:05:24,885
In Detroit, his parents were told he had

136
00:05:24,905 --> 00:05:26,162
been killed in action.

137
00:05:26,182 --> 00:05:29,033
It took about a month for a Red Cross

138
00:05:29,053 --> 00:05:31,586
letter to tell them he was a prisoner.

139
00:05:31,606 --> 00:05:34,777
By then he had met the man with the book.

140
00:05:34,797 --> 00:05:37,329
For what was in it, we have only

141
00:05:37,349 --> 00:05:39,244
Jefferson's word, given in a radio

142
00:05:39,264 --> 00:05:40,540
interview sixty years later.

143
00:05:42,175 --> 00:05:44,555
A train took him to Stalag Luft Three,

144
00:05:44,575 --> 00:05:47,291
near Sagan, about a hundred miles

145
00:05:47,311 --> 00:05:48,428
southeast of Berlin.

146
00:05:48,448 --> 00:05:51,459
It is the camp of the Great Escape.

147
00:05:51,479 --> 00:05:55,135
He arrived in September 1944.

148
00:05:55,155 --> 00:05:57,630
It held about five thousand American officers.

149
00:05:57,650 --> 00:06:01,215
By his own count, he was the fourth Black one.

150
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:05,477
The Army that sent him had trained him

151
00:06:05,497 --> 00:06:07,350
apart, at a separate airfield,

152
00:06:07,370 --> 00:06:08,849
in an all-Black group.

153
00:06:08,869 --> 00:06:11,097
Here there was no such line.

154
00:06:11,117 --> 00:06:14,094
He got the same Red Cross clothing as

155
00:06:14,114 --> 00:06:16,716
everyone else, and the same short rations.

156
00:06:16,736 --> 00:06:20,088
In his words, he had all the rights and

157
00:06:20,108 --> 00:06:21,961
privileges of an American officer.

158
00:06:21,981 --> 00:06:23,480
No segregation, no discrimination.

159
00:06:24,975 --> 00:06:27,720
The men followed the war on a hidden radio

160
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:28,948
tuned to the BBC.

161
00:06:28,968 --> 00:06:31,406
And new prisoners kept coming through the gate.

162
00:06:31,426 --> 00:06:33,249
One day a bomber crew arrived,

163
00:06:33,269 --> 00:06:35,706
and one of them looked at Jefferson and

164
00:06:35,726 --> 00:06:38,470
told him that if the Red Tails had been

165
00:06:38,490 --> 00:06:41,255
with them, they would not have been shot down.

166
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:44,835
Just before midnight on the twenty-seventh

167
00:06:44,855 --> 00:06:48,749
of January, 1945, with Soviet troops about

168
00:06:48,769 --> 00:06:50,998
sixteen miles away, the Germans emptied

169
00:06:51,018 --> 00:06:51,747
the camp.

170
00:06:51,767 --> 00:06:54,371
About eleven thousand men walked out into

171
00:06:54,391 --> 00:06:55,890
six inches of snow.

172
00:06:57,200 --> 00:06:59,011
Jefferson remembered eighty kilometers on

173
00:06:59,031 --> 00:07:00,843
foot, and twenty below zero.

174
00:07:00,862 --> 00:07:04,139
At the end of the road they were loaded

175
00:07:04,159 --> 00:07:06,703
into cattle cars and taken south to

176
00:07:06,722 --> 00:07:08,168
Bavaria, to Stalag Seven-A,

177
00:07:08,188 --> 00:07:08,988
at Moosburg.

178
00:07:10,225 --> 00:07:14,028
That camp had opened in 1939 for up to ten

179
00:07:14,048 --> 00:07:15,170
thousand Polish prisoners.

180
00:07:15,190 --> 00:07:17,835
By the end, more than seventy-six thousand

181
00:07:17,855 --> 00:07:19,358
men were in it.

182
00:07:19,378 --> 00:07:22,805
Jefferson spent his last months of the war there.

183
00:07:24,025 --> 00:07:25,765
On the twenty-ninth of April,

184
00:07:25,785 --> 00:07:27,526
the American fourteenth Armored Division,

185
00:07:27,546 --> 00:07:29,286
part of Patton's Third Army,

186
00:07:29,306 --> 00:07:31,047
came up to the camp.

187
00:07:31,067 --> 00:07:33,512
After a short fight at the bridges,

188
00:07:33,532 --> 00:07:35,976
the two hundred and forty guards surrendered.

189
00:07:35,996 --> 00:07:38,441
Jefferson had been a prisoner for nearly

190
00:07:38,461 --> 00:07:39,261
nine months.

191
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:43,834
The next day, somebody told him there was

192
00:07:43,854 --> 00:07:47,964
a place down the road with a lot of dead people.

193
00:07:47,984 --> 00:07:51,344
He and some others found a jeep and drove

194
00:07:51,364 --> 00:07:52,470
to see it.

195
00:07:52,490 --> 00:07:53,597
It was Dachau.

196
00:07:53,617 --> 00:07:56,225
He said the ovens were still warm.

197
00:07:56,245 --> 00:07:59,229
He said he would never forget the smell.

198
00:07:59,249 --> 00:08:01,858
He remembered a long table covered with

199
00:08:01,878 --> 00:08:02,609
gold teeth.

200
00:08:02,629 --> 00:08:05,988
Sixty years later, he had one phrase for it.

201
00:08:06,008 --> 00:08:07,510
Man's inhumanity to man.

202
00:08:09,200 --> 00:08:12,440
A transport plane took him to Le Havre.

203
00:08:12,460 --> 00:08:14,005
A ship took him to England,

204
00:08:14,025 --> 00:08:16,092
and from there he crossed the Atlantic on

205
00:08:16,112 --> 00:08:17,657
the Queen Mary, into New York,

206
00:08:17,677 --> 00:08:20,325
in the middle of 1945.

207
00:08:21,025 --> 00:08:23,509
He walked down the gangplank in an

208
00:08:23,529 --> 00:08:24,225
officer's uniform.

209
00:08:24,245 --> 00:08:27,087
In front of him was a big sign.

210
00:08:27,107 --> 00:08:29,948
Whites to the right, colored to the left.

211
00:08:29,968 --> 00:08:32,810
A white soldier at the bottom pointed the

212
00:08:32,830 --> 00:08:36,388
way, and he used a racial slur to do it.

213
00:08:36,408 --> 00:08:38,892
Jefferson had a phrase for that too.

214
00:08:38,912 --> 00:08:39,985
Back to segregation.

215
00:08:41,475 --> 00:08:43,767
When NPR told his story in 2006,

216
00:08:43,787 --> 00:08:45,419
it put the matter simply.

217
00:08:45,439 --> 00:08:48,061
He had been given more respect as a

218
00:08:48,081 --> 00:08:51,034
prisoner of the Nazis than as a Black man

219
00:08:51,054 --> 00:08:51,854
in America.

220
00:08:53,500 --> 00:08:56,200
He went back to Tuskegee as an instrument

221
00:08:56,220 --> 00:09:00,400
instructor until the field closed in 1946.

222
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:02,482
Two years later, on the twenty-sixth of

223
00:09:02,502 --> 00:09:05,833
July, 1948, President Truman ordered the

224
00:09:05,853 --> 00:09:07,520
armed forces to end segregation.

225
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,618
Jefferson went home to Detroit and taught

226
00:09:11,638 --> 00:09:13,879
elementary school science for more than

227
00:09:13,899 --> 00:09:14,633
thirty years.

228
00:09:14,653 --> 00:09:18,024
He stayed in the reserves and retired as a

229
00:09:18,044 --> 00:09:18,778
lieutenant colonel.

230
00:09:18,798 --> 00:09:22,169
His Purple Heart, for the day he was shot

231
00:09:22,189 --> 00:09:24,807
down, came in 2004, sixty years later.

232
00:09:24,827 --> 00:09:27,068
In 2005 he published his memoir,

233
00:09:27,088 --> 00:09:30,480
with the sketches he had made as a prisoner.

234
00:09:31,825 --> 00:09:34,024
In 2006, Congress voted the Tuskegee

235
00:09:34,044 --> 00:09:36,982
Airmen its gold medal, and the law counted

236
00:09:37,002 --> 00:09:37,721
the cost.

237
00:09:37,741 --> 00:09:40,309
Sixty-six of these pilots were killed in

238
00:09:40,329 --> 00:09:42,158
combat, while another thirty-two were

239
00:09:42,178 --> 00:09:44,746
either forced down or shot down and

240
00:09:44,766 --> 00:09:46,985
captured to become prisoners of war.

241
00:09:48,275 --> 00:09:50,604
Near the end of that NPR interview,

242
00:09:50,624 --> 00:09:52,952
Jefferson gave his own verdict on the

243
00:09:52,972 --> 00:09:55,301
country at the bottom of the gangplank.

244
00:09:55,321 --> 00:09:58,320
He called it the best country in the world.

245
00:09:58,340 --> 00:09:59,998
The only obligation, he said,

246
00:10:00,018 --> 00:10:01,675
was to make it better.

247
00:10:01,695 --> 00:10:03,353
It wasn't perfect, he said,

248
00:10:03,373 --> 00:10:04,715
but it was home.

249
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,340
If your family has a story about the

250
00:10:08,360 --> 00:10:10,110
Tuskegee Airmen, or about an American

251
00:10:10,130 --> 00:10:11,585
prisoner in a German camp,

252
00:10:11,605 --> 00:10:13,080
tell us in the comments.

253
00:10:14,850 --> 00:10:17,959
Alexander Jefferson died in Detroit on the

254
00:10:17,979 --> 00:10:19,748
twenty-second of June, 2022.

255
00:10:19,768 --> 00:10:22,450
He was one hundred years old.

256
00:10:23,650 --> 00:10:26,604
Two years later, the city put up a bronze

257
00:10:26,624 --> 00:10:28,587
statue of him in Rouge Park,

258
00:10:28,607 --> 00:10:30,901
on a field that carries his name.

259
00:10:30,921 --> 00:10:33,545
The local club still flies model airplanes there.

260
00:10:33,565 --> 00:10:35,197
He flew them there himself,

261
00:10:35,217 --> 00:10:36,189
late in life.

262
00:10:36,209 --> 00:10:38,502
So the boy who built model warplanes

263
00:10:38,522 --> 00:10:41,146
stands at the edge of an airfield again,

264
00:10:41,166 --> 00:10:43,810
and the small planes go over his head.

